19:37:24 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That's a completely fair point, and your real‑world experience trumps any theoretical explanation. If you pre‑dosed with ibuprofen and your knees still killed you by the end of the hike, then clearly something else is going on that my previous “bouncer at the club” story didn't cover. Let me adjust the model based on your feedback, because you've hit on a crucial limitation of **what kind of knee pain** can be helped by pre‑dosing. The short version: many types of knee pain during and after a hike aren't primarily driven by cyclooxygenase‑made prostaglandins—or they involve a mechanical, structural stress that no pill can silence in real time. Ibuprofen can only turn down one specific volume knob, and in your case that knob probably wasn't the main one. Let’s break down why your knees still hurt despite the pre‑dose. ### 1. Mechanical overload doesn’t care about prostaglandins When you hike downhill or carry a pack, your knee is subjected to huge forces. If you have a bit of cartilage wear, a slightly misaligned kneecap, tight IT bands, or even just muscle fatigue that changes your gait, certain structures get physically compressed, stretched, or rubbed in a way they aren't used to. That type of pain—often a deep, burning ache inside the joint or a sharp "catch"—is a direct mechanical stimulation of nerve endings inside the bone, synovium, or tendons. Those nerve signals are transmitted by the same fast‑and‑slow fibers I mentioned before, but they're firing because of physical deformation, not because a chemical like prostaglandin is sensitizing them. Ibuprofen won't stop a bone bruise or a frayed meniscus from being pinched any more than it would stop a hammer from hurting. ### 2. Your pain may have been the “slow‑burn” nociceptive ache, not purely inflammatory This is a key nuance. Prostaglandins are not the only chemical that makes sore muscles and joints ache. When tissues are overworked, they can accumulate metabolites like acid, pot